; Install-script policy. See docs/adr/0005-supply-chain-policy.md.
;
; The list of what may run code at install time is NOT here — it lives in
; package.json under `allowScripts`. npm's own configuration documentation says
; this file's `allow-scripts` key is meant for one-off and global contexts
; (`npx`, `npm install -g`), and that passing `--allow-scripts` during a
; project-scoped `npm ci` is an error. The package.json field is also the only
; one of the two that can express an explicit *denial*, which is what all four
; of this project's entries are.
;
; What this line does: turns npm's "N packages had install scripts blocked"
; warning into a hard failure (`ESTRICTALLOWSCRIPTS`, exit 1). A new dependency
; that wants to run code at install time then has to be approved or denied
; deliberately, with `npm install-scripts approve` or `npm install-scripts deny`,
; instead of being waved through by a warning nobody reads.
;
; IT ONLY WORKS ON npm 12 OR LATER. This was bisected: no npm 11.x release
; understands the key. Worse, npm 10 accepts it from this file and reports it as
; `true` while doing nothing at all — so the presence of this line is not
; evidence that it applied. npm 12 also requires Node >= 22.22.2, which is above
; this project's supported Node floor, so it cannot simply be required of
; everyone. The `supply-chain` CI job is the one place it is genuinely enforced.
; See docs/dev/ci.md for what that means for the rest of the matrix.
;
; This file must never gain an auth token. Publishing credentials belong in the
; release workflow's environment, not in a committed file.
strict-allow-scripts=true
